  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Low humidity and precipitation chances until the \nweekend, when chances for showers and thunderstorms return and \nlinger through early-mid next week.\n\nAloft, a trough will linger over the region through Thu, then \ngradually shift ewd and offshore Thu night/Fri as the parent low \nlifts newd across New England and into the Maritimes. A s/w will \nride the ne periphery of the anti-cyclone anchored over the Desert \nSouthwest, dropping sse from the nrn Plains on Thu to the TN/OH \nValley region by Sat/Sat night and amplifying the trough over the \nern CONUS. While details become less clear over the weekend, the s/w \nshould shear out a bit and lift newd over the weekend/early next \nweek. At the surface, between the departing low near New England and \nthe high moving into/across the OH Valley, relatively cool, dry air \nwill continue to advect swd along the Appalachians/across the mid-\nAtlantic through Thu night. The high should gradually weaken over \nthe OH Valley/n-cent Appalachians on Fri, while the low lifts newd \nover the nrn Atlantic. The next low pressure system will strengthen \nas it moves across the cent Plains and mid MS-Valley Fri and Sat, \nwith its progression thereafter still a bit uncertain. The weather \nshould be mainly dry through Friday, with chances for showers and \nstorms returning as the s/w approaches from the west over the \nweekend. Without a fropa or significant airmass change, expect \nchances for showers and storms to linger into early-mid next week, \nthough confidence decreases beyond this weekend.",
